1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,800
Menopause joint pain: if your knees ache going down the stairs and your fingers feel stiff every morning, save this.

2
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:13,280
Estrogen protects cartilage, calms inflammation in the joints, and keeps ligaments flexible.

3
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:22,560
Once it drops after menopause, more than half of women develop joint pain, and those who never used hormone therapy show nearly triple the risk of osteoarthritis.

4
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:28,240
Three habits matter most here: the muscles around your joints, your weight, and omega-3s.

5
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:30,800
First, the muscles around your joints.

6
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:37,120
Weaker muscles can't absorb shock from walking or stairs, so that impact lands directly on the joint.

7
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:41,920
Start with light strength training for your thighs and calves, and build up gradually.

8
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:43,680
Second, your weight.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,760
Extra weight multiplies the load on your knees, which is why going down stairs often hurts most.

10
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:55,600
Low-impact cardio like walking or swimming helps bring that weight down without adding more stress.

11
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:57,440
Finally, omega-3s.

12
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:03,440
Skipping fatty fish means missing fats that calm joint inflammation, so pain tends to stick around longer.

13
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:08,640
Add salmon or mackerel to your meals, or fill the gap with a supplement.

14
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:12,560
Keep up these three habits together, and the pain noticeably eases.
